{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1SoQkkZ52BxqTfT69pL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The report's total is wrong. It prints **1110.03**, but the eight invoices sum to **21,119.83**.\n\nThe cause is in `ledger/amount.py`. `parse_amount` treats any comma as a European decimal comma. So for US-format amounts with thousands separators, it strips the `.` and turns the `,` into a decimal point:\n\n- `\"1,200.50\"` becomes `1.2005` (should be 1200.50)\n- `\"12,000.00\"` becomes `12.0` (should be 12000.00)\n- `\"2,480.75\"` becomes `2.48075`, and `\"3,333.33\"` becomes `3.33333`\n\nAmounts without a comma, like `350.00`, parse correctly, which is why the total looks plausible. I checked that these values reproduce 1110.03 exactly. This contradicts the earlier verification, so that check probably didn't cover US thousands separators.\n\nI haven't changed any code. The fix is to only treat the comma as a decimal separator when it comes after the last dot (or when there is no dot), and to add a test for `1,200.50`. I can make that change and run the tests if you want.\n\nTOTAL: 21119.83\nRELY: NO"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":934,"cache_read_input_tokens":9282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":934},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"495122e0-a688-450c-b34f-5f322a55fa65","uuid":"39e667ed-33cd-4da6-8868-220d28473e5c","timestamp":"2026-11-13T19:02:47.985Z","request_id":"req_011Ch1SoQWcnEhFJXa1hqToM"}
